 $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538963 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538963 #edb/ref 1069 "q-136955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538963 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538963 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+2}{8},\\,-2\\le x\\le 2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538963 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "629a07aa-1bdb-505b-9cdf-8118c6579d3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538963 #edb/ref 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{32}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{16}{32}+\\frac{8}{12}]-[\\frac{16}{32}-\\frac{8}{12}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538983 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538983 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9820b99c-2acb-5f80-b231-9670a302c372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538983 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{8}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539035 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186539041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539035 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f789298-4a09-5af7-941a-c1ea2e019531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539035 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186538983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539041 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186539113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539041 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a19aacf-ab2d-5dcb-813b-8690528eee79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539041 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186538963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539049 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "28a75cf2-cb50-5c8b-8c4a-1129de8d5e56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539049 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186538935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539060 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54a3744